A||||4||cmyokley||Dist./Alt. Fan Blower||||1||
Z||000000||cmyokley||12-02-1999||05:16 PM||||How important to the function of an 86GT is the fan unit that blows cool air onto the dist. and alt. of the V6? I have heard that the 87 and 88 did not have this blower...is this true???...if so is there anything different in the dist. and alt. that would make them less subseptable to heating problems?<P>The reason I am asking is that I have removed the unit from my car after putting side scoops on and removing the screens from the deck louvers. I figured this mod would remove more heat and therefor I could remove the blower. Now I have a problem with my car that is strange. I can drive the car without problem but if I stop to fill up the gas tank or go shopping when I get back the car will crank but gets no fire from the coil. Then after 30 minutes or so something cools down and the car will start right up.<P>So I can deduce that an electrical component is heating up after the car is turned off from radinant heat and will only work again when the electrical component cools off. I think the electrical component is either the coil or the pick up coil in the dist.<P>I can always put the air blower back on but would like to know why some V6's do not need them.<P>Any help?||||reg||1
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||12-02-1999||08:19 PM||||I put a 2.8 in my 88 Coupe without the blower motor. There were no holes in the bulkhead to install it, so I left it out. I have driven it most of the summer, in close to 100 degree heat, with no problems so far.||||reg||4
Z||000002||AL||12-02-1999||08:24 PM||||sounds like a coil or module is going bad.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||12-02-1999||09:01 PM||||I agree with Al, sounds like a defective ignition part.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||12-03-1999||04:15 AM||||In 88 the crossover pipe were completly shrouded with a heat shield. In 85-87 the pipe is exposed and without the shields the coil is sitting right on top of pipes. The shield allowed them to get away without the blowers. The alternator was protected by a heat sheild on the manifold instead of the protective shield on the alternator itself. The downside of the 88? The shroud does not let moisture escape so the crossover pipe is more likely to crack than the 85-87. As ugly as they are, I leave my blower pipes on so I don't have to find out how many coils I have to lose from heat soaking. The pipes blow a significant amount of air, just turn on your AC, the pipes blow when the fan goes on. You will feel the air. <P>So, you might want to put them back on.   There just isn't enough air moving from air scoops and vents to keep it cool, especially in regular stop and go street driving.<P>Or....<P>You could get an aftermarket coil and bolt it to the trunk firewall down low and close to the strut tower. Really cleans up that area without the coil.<P>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-03-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-03-1999).]||||reg||1
